{
"Amitābha vowed that all who sincerely wish to be born in his land and call his name will be born there.": "dd7c12cce97a",
"As the mind is purified, the buddha land is purified. Finally, self-power and other-power are not two.": "96c27fc37ae4",
"Faith was part of Buddhism from the start. It heads the list of the five faculties, faith, energy, mindfulness, immersion, and wisdom, and at the end of one discourse the Buddha says, in Bhikkhu Sujatos translation, that those who have a degree of faith and love for me are all bound for heaven (Majjhima Nikāya 22). The Mahāyāna Pure Land sūtras made faith central. In the Sūtra of Immeasurable Life, the monk Dharmākara makes forty-eight vows before becoming the buddha Amitābha. The eighteenth, in Saṃghavarmans Chinese translation, reads: If, when I attain buddhahood, the beings of the ten directions, with sincere faith and joy, wish to be born in my land and call on me even ten times, and are not born there, may I not attain perfect awakening, excepting only those who commit the five grave offenses and slander the true Dharma. Since he did become a buddha, the tradition reasons, the vow is fulfilled. The shorter Amitābha Sūtra adds the practice: hold to his name for one day, two days seven days, with a single mind undisturbed.": "11ecccf47bf4",
"Luthers Concerning Christian Liberty sets out a view of faith and works that Pure Land readers find startlingly familiar. In the translation by Wace and Buchheim: Faith alone and the efficacious use of the word of God, bring salvation. Works do not make a person good. True, then, are these two sayings: Good works do not make a good man, but a good man does good works. And the treatise opens with a paradox: A Christian man is the most free lord of all, and subject to none; a Christian man is the most dutiful servant of all, and subject to every one. The structure matches Tanluan and Shinran closely. Human effort cannot reach the goal, the goal is given through trust in anothers power, and the good life flows from that trust and does not earn it. Christian theologians have noticed the parallel with Shinran, and some have been unsettled by it. The difference is the one who gives. Luthers God is the creator and judge of all, who justifies the sinner. Amitābha is neither creator nor judge.": "22c8629b9dcb",
"Nāgārjuna said some paths are like walking over land, and some are like sailing in a boat.": "df90c015e319",
"Pascal argued that one cannot stand outside the question of faith. In W. F. Trotters translation of the Pensées: Yes; but you must wager. It is not optional. You are embarked. Then the calculation: Let us weigh the gain and the loss in wagering that God is. If you gain, you gain all; if you lose, you lose nothing. Wager, then, without hesitation that He is. He also knew that argument alone does not produce faith. The heart has its reasons, which reason does not know, and It is the heart which experiences God, and not the reason. Pure Land teachers made similar moves. The image of being already embarked fits Nāgārjunas boat, and the tradition also says that calling the name with a sincere heart matters more than understanding the doctrine. The difference is in what is staked. Pascals wager concerns whether God exists at all. The Pure Land practitioner is not asked to bet on a creator. They are asked to trust a buddhas vow and to act on it, and the tradition claims that the trust bears fruit in a softer, more grateful life now.": "bc7a992f4f75",
"Tanluan compared other-power to a weak man who cannot climb onto a donkey but rides through the sky in a kings procession.": "68393e61b282",
"The Chinese master Tanluan, in the sixth century, turned this into a teaching about two kinds of power. On the hard path, one difficulty is that it is self-power alone, without the support of other-power. The easy path is simply, through faith in the Buddha, to wish to be born in the Pure Land, and riding on the Buddhas vow-power, to be born there. His illustration is memorable. A person who keeps the precepts, then develops concentration, then supernormal powers, and so travels the four continents, does it by self-power. A weak man who cannot even climb onto a donkey, if he follows the procession of a wheel-turning king, rides through the sky and travels the four continents without obstruction. This is called other-power. Japanese teachers, above all Shinran in the thirteenth century, pushed the idea further, until even the faith was understood as Amitābhas gift. In Chinese Buddhism the emphasis falls on faith, vow, and practice together, and the chapter called The True Cause of Rebirth, in Xia Lianjus compiled edition of the Sūtra of Immeasurable Life, promises that even busy people with families can be born there.": "162dbe1d47b7",
"The Danish philosopher Søren Kierkegaard insisted that faith is not the conclusion of an argument and cannot be reached by piling up evidence. It is a decision made with the whole of ones existence, in the face of uncertainty, and for that reason it is a passion rather than a piece of knowledge. In the Concluding Unscientific Postscript he described truth, for an existing person, as a matter of how one relates to what one believes, and he mocked the scholar who postpones commitment until the research is complete. The popular phrase leap of faith summarizes his view, though he did not use exactly those words. The parallel to the Pure Land is in the role of the whole person. Shinrans faith is not assent to propositions, and it is meant to transform how one lives. The difference is in the tone. Kierkegaards faith is lonely and full of dread, a single self before God. The Pure Lands faith is meant to bring relief from exactly that burden, the weak man carried along in the kings procession.": "5a4905be36ca",
"The Pure Land is a place to finish the path to awakening. It is not the end of the story.": "08e38819d246",
"The closest is Luther. Both he and the Pure Land masters begin from the conviction that ordinary people cannot save themselves by effort, both place the turning point in trust in anothers power, and both say that good conduct follows from that trust and does not purchase it. Pascal is closest on the predicament of being already embarked, and on the place of the heart. Kierkegaard is closest on faith as an act of the whole person, which no argument can replace.": "e02d29a99be9",
"The first further step is that the Pure Land is a means. In Christian thought, salvation is the end of the story, eternal life with God. In the Pure Land sūtras, birth in Amitābhas land is a beginning. The vows promise that those born there will not fall back and will reach awakening, and some vows promise that they will return to help others. The Pure Land is a better school, and in Tanluans image the weak man is carried along in the kings procession so that he can travel without obstruction. This changes the meaning of faith. It is not trust that someone will save you from judgment. It is trust that conditions for awakening can be given to those who cannot make them for themselves. A Western teacher can present the Pure Land honestly as compassion built into the structure of the path, and need not call it a Buddhist heaven.": "8da76b71a22c",
"The idea of an easier path goes back to Nāgārjuna, or at least to the Ten Stages Treatise traditionally attributed to him. A questioner asks whether there is an easy way to reach the stage of no return. The treatise first scolds him: this is the talk of the timid and inferior, not the resolve of a great person. Then it gives the easy way anyway. The Buddhas teaching has countless gates. As with the roads of the world, some are hard and some are easy. Walking by land is hard; traveling by water in a boat is pleasant. The bodhisattva path is the same. Some practice with diligent effort, and some, by the skillful means of faith, practice the easy way and quickly reach the stage of no return. The easy way is to call respectfully on the names of the buddhas.": "b208920406f1",
"The likeness ends at what is trusted. Amitābha is not the creator of the world, he does not judge sins, and he does not forgive them as a judge forgives. He was a bodhisattva named Dharmākara whose vows took effect because he fulfilled them through practice, and his power works, in Tanluans word, as a dominant condition, one cause among the causes of a beings awakening. Tanluan says, If it were not for the Buddhas power, the forty-eight vows would have been made in vain, and the power is still part of the web of conditions the Buddha taught. And the goal is the same as on every Buddhist path. The Pure Land is a place where awakening becomes easy, and it is not the final salvation of a soul. Later Chinese teachers drew the conclusion, following the Vimalakīrti Sūtra: If a bodhisattva wishes to attain a pure land, he should purify his mind. As the mind is purified, the buddha land is purified. In the end self-power and other-power are not two.": "79442f27fff8",
"The second further step is that self-power and other-power finally meet. Shinran stressed other-power so strongly that even faith became Amitābhas gift, and some critics thought this made practice pointless. The Chinese tradition kept faith, vow, and practice together. In The True Cause of Rebirth the sūtra asks busy householders to keep the ten good deeds, to think of Amitābha day and night, and to be filial and obedient, sincere and loyal. And Mahāyāna philosophy dissolves the opposition from the other side. If all things are empty and interdependent, there is no fixed self to save itself and no fixed other to do the saving. The Vimalakīrti Sūtras line, as the mind is purified, the buddha land is purified, puts the Pure Land in the purified mind. This is why Chinese masters could say that Chan and Pure Land are one practice, which the next lesson takes up.": "6c5a4fae059d",
"The structure resembles Luthers faith alone, but Amitābha is not a creator or a judge.": "f0259e24db18",
"Westerners who know Buddhism mainly as meditation are often surprised by the Pure Land, and those raised Protestant may say at once, Thats salvation by grace. Both reactions are useful. Start with Nāgārjunas two roads, walking by land and sailing by water, because it shows that the easy path was presented from the beginning as one gate among many and was never offered as a shortcut for the lazy. Then tell Tanluans story of the weak man and the kings procession. Agree with the Protestant listener that the structure is like Luthers, and then name the difference: Amitābha is not a creator and does not judge, and the Pure Land is a place to finish the path. End with the Vimalakīrti Sūtras line, which brings the whole teaching back to the mind.": "8afe35246a28"
}
